𝐃𝐞𝐯𝐎𝐩𝐬 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐞𝐰 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐜𝐞𝐭𝐩𝐬:

❓𝐄𝐍𝐕 𝐯𝐬 𝐀𝐑𝐆 𝐢𝐧 𝐃𝐨𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐫𝐟𝐢𝐥𝐞.

🧩 1️⃣ 𝐏𝐮𝐫𝐩𝐨𝐬𝐞 — 𝐁𝐮𝐢𝐥𝐝-𝐓𝐢𝐦𝐞 𝐯𝐬 𝐑𝐮𝐧-𝐓𝐢𝐦𝐞

🔧 ARG → Build-Time Variable
Used only while building the Docker image.
Once the image is built, ARG disappears.

🌍 ENV → Run-Time Variable
Lives inside the final container and is available during and after the build.

💡 Think of it as:
ARG = temporary construction material
ENV = permanent furniture in the house

🏗️ 2️⃣ 𝐖𝐡𝐞𝐫𝐞 𝐓𝐡𝐞𝐲 𝐋𝐢𝐯𝐞 — 𝐕𝐢𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲 𝐒𝐜𝐨𝐩𝐞

ARG
• Available only in Dockerfile during build
• NOT available inside the final image (unless copied to ENV)

ENV
• Available to the container at runtime
• Visible to your application, scripts, processes, shells

✔ ENV persists
✘ ARG evaporates

🔒 3️⃣ 𝐒𝐞𝐜𝐮𝐫𝐢𝐭𝐲 — 𝐖𝐡𝐚𝐭 𝐍𝐨𝐭 𝐭𝐨 𝐔𝐬𝐞

❌ Never store secrets in ARG or ENV
Both end up in the image history (docker history) or environment.

➡ But if you must pass a temp value (like a build flag), use ARG, because ENV persists longer.

🔄 4️⃣ 𝐔𝐬𝐞 𝐂𝐚𝐬𝐞 𝐅𝐢𝐭 — 𝐖𝐡𝐞𝐧 𝐭𝐨 𝐔𝐬𝐞 𝐖𝐡𝐚𝐭
ARG — Use When:

• Installing OS-level packages
• Choosing tool versions at build
• Passing build flags
• Multi-stage build decisions

Example:

ARG NODE_VERSION=18
FROM node:${NODE_VERSION}-slim

ENV — Use When:

• Setting app configs
• Defining default runtime values
• Exposing app settings

Example:

ENV NODE_ENV=production

🚀 5️⃣ 𝐁𝐞𝐬𝐭 𝐏𝐫𝐚𝐜𝐭𝐢𝐜𝐞 — 𝐓𝐡𝐞 𝐇𝐲𝐛𝐫𝐢𝐝 𝐀𝐩𝐩𝐫𝐨𝐚𝐜𝐡

The most professional Dockerfiles use ARG → ENV handoff:

ARG APP_ENV=production
ENV APP_ENV=${APP_ENV}

🔥𝐆𝐨𝐥𝐝𝐞𝐧 𝐑𝐮𝐥𝐞:
👉 Use ARG for building the image.
👉 Use ENV for running the container.
